n months we've  been calling on people on both sides to  condemn  violence where it occurs and in the  middle of the  in the middle of the problem at the  capitol on wednesday we did get that  tweet from the president  calling on people to leave the capitol  to be peaceful to respect the police  officers and so forth  um so that was obviously helpful it was  a stronger statement yesterday  and i would note that this  administration is pursuing justice  against those folks  and unlike other parts of the country  portland is an obvious comparison  the district of columbia the federal  government in many respects acts like  the local government so  unlike say the prosecutor in portland  who lets lots of rioters go without  charges  that is not happening here the fbi is  fully pursuing  anyone they can find who is involved in  this criminal activity  and we at dhs are assisting them in that  they have the point  and there's no restraint on that we are  fully  across the administration pursuing every  avenue of justice available  why was the response so slow to this mr  secretary  well that's an excellent question and  the capitol police  are in a unique position the only other  similar  police force in the district of columbia  is the supreme court police  the capitol police report only to  congress  and the supreme court police report only  to the court  it is that branch of government's own  police force  we were moving uh folks secret service  agents in particular  over before the violence began but that  has to come  at the request of the capitol police  because they have jurisdiction there  and so when they made that request we  moved as quickly as we could and secret  service agents federal protective  service agents  moved to support them and in literally  in the hundreds  so uh that that did happen but the  problem of course is  the initial breach and there was no  backup behind that  and it does appear that there was  inadequate preparation  and numbers by the 